    {
      "filename": "Fossil trail of giant myriapod - geograph.org.uk - 854033.jpg",
      "title": "File:Fossil trail of giant myriapod - geograph.org.uk - 854033.jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7c/Fossil_trail_of_giant_myriapod_-_geograph.org.uk_-_854033.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fossil_trail_of_giant_myriapod_-_geograph.org.uk_-_854033.jpg",
      "width": 640,
      "height": 606,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 2.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0",
      "artist": "Keith Burns",
      "description": "Fossil trail of giant myriapod of the family Arthropleuridae near Laggan, Isle of Arran, Scotland. Two parallel fossil tracks running down and left from the pointing fingers. These are on a sloping slab of Carboniferous sandstone (about 300 Million years old) on the RH side of a narrow shingly inlet close to the “Duchess Anne’s salt pan” building.[1] For discussion of arthropleurid trace fossils (this one belongs to the ichnogenus Diplichnites), see Briggs et al. (1979).[2]\nNot visible, by the camera position, there is a 9in. coal seam outcropping. These local coal exposures provided the incentive to evaporate sea water for its salt in the salt pans (from 1710 to 1735).[1]",
      "dateCreated": "2008-06-18",
      "sourceType": "commons-category",
      "sourceArticleId": "lochranza-wp",
      "sourceGeohash4": "gcuh"
    },
